---
import Prose from './Prose.astro';
import CodeBlock from './CodeBlock.astro';
import Callout from './Callout.astro';
import { CI_YML } from '../data/guide';
---

<Prose class="max-w-none">
  <p>
    TechCart has one GitHub Actions workflow, and it does continuous integration only — no
    deployment. Here it is, trimmed:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock title=".github/workflows/ci.yml" lang="yaml" code={CI_YML} />

  <h3>The three jobs</h3>
  <p>
    The workflow triggers on every pull request to main. A concurrency group with cancel-in-progress
    means a fresh push supersedes an in-flight run. All jobs pin Node with node-version-file
    pointing at .nvmrc and install with npm ci.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>lint</strong> — runs npm run lint across the whole repo.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>test</strong> — a matrix over backend, buyer-app and admin-app. The backend leg caches
      the mongodb-binaries download and reads the ATLAS_SEARCH_TEST_URI secret.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>build</strong> — the same matrix; the buyer-app build gets dummy NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL
      and NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID values.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>Branch protection marks all three as required, so main only ever receives green code.</p>

  <h3>Why CD is not a GitHub Actions job</h3>
  <p>
    Render watches the repository through its render.yaml Blueprint and Vercel watches it natively;
    both deploy on merge to main on their own. Putting deployment into Actions as well would
    duplicate that work, require storing platform deploy tokens as CI secrets, and give up the
    platforms' automatic per-PR preview deployments.
  </p>
  <Callout type="tip">
    Continuous integration lives in GitHub Actions; continuous delivery is owned by the hosting
    platforms. Keeping them separate is deliberate, not an omission.
  </Callout>

  <h3>Recommended approach</h3>
  <p>
    Keep the CI workflow small and fast — lint, test and build on every pull request — and let the
    managed platform own delivery through its native git integration.
  </p>
</Prose>
